      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Director Douglas Cheek tapped into legitimate fears about nuclear waste disposal and urban homeless populations, casting respected character actors John Heard and Daniel Stern to ground the outlandish premise. The practical effects by Ed French deliver memorably grotesque creatures that hold up surprisingly well, while the grimy location shooting in actual New York tunnels adds documentary-like authenticity similar to <a href=\"/movie/8333\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"8333\">The Taking of Pelham One Two Three</a> (1974)."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The film spawned a lesser sequel but its influence extends through later toxic mutation films like <a href=\"/movie/1255\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"1255\">The Host</a> (2006) and <a href=\"/movie/43933\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"43933\">Monsters</a> (2010). Its blend of social consciousness and horror anticipates later works like <a href=\"/movie/8337\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"8337\">They Live</a> (1988) and <a href=\"/movie/13122\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"13122\">The People Under the Stairs</a> (1991) that use genre tropes to critique class inequality and governmental corruption. SUBHEAD: Urban Decay and Monster Movies"
      },
      {
        "type": "movies",
        "movies": [
          {
