          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "C.H.U.D. emerged during a fertile period of New York-set horror that included <a href=\"/movie/10725\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"10725\">Wolfen</a> (1981), <a href=\"/movie/20980\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"20980\">Liquid Sky</a> (1982) and <a href=\"/movie/620\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"620\">Ghostbusters</a> (1984). These films transformed the city's crime and decay into supernatural threats while often incorporating documentary-style footage of genuine urban blight. The underground settings tap into primal fears while serving as metaphors for society's forgotten populations, similar to later films like <a href=\"/movie/4961\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"4961\">Mimic</a> (1997) and <a href=\"/movie/14273\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"14273\">Dark Days</a> (2000)."
      }
